  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nAn active weather pattern remains in place across the Ohio Valley, as\na stalled frontal boundary provides a focus for repeated rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms. One round of storms is currently impacting\nsouthern Ohio with significantly heavy rainfall. Precipitable water\nvalues of over 2 inches are capable of sustaining these kinds of\nrainfall rates. Attention will then turn to a round of storms\ncurrently over southern Illinois, which will probably clip the\nsouthern part of the ILN forecast area during the early morning.\n\nA third round is forecast to impact the southern part of the forecast\narea from mid to late afternoon, with higher confidence in this round\noccurring and producing heavy rainfall. Still not seeing enough\nconfidence in storm placement for the next few rounds to issue a\nflood watch, but will continue to communicate the concerns for\nmultiple rounds of storms in a moisture-primed environment. SPC has \nalso placed a MRGL risk in the far southern sections of the forecast \narea, with some potential for strong to severe winds, though thin \nCAPE profiles and marginal shear will make this threat rather \nlimited.\n\nWith quite a bit of cloud cover expected today, highs will be in the\nupper 70s to around 80 -- slightly below normal for early to mid\nSeptember.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nThe frontal boundary will drift just to the south of the area on \nFriday, leaving most (if not all) of the forecast area dry. An \napproaching shortwave on Saturday will lead to another surge of \nmoisture northward into the Ohio Valley, along with a chance for some\nshowers and storms.\n\nWarming conditions are expected from Sunday through the middle of\nnext week, with temperatures reaching values above normal.",
